Bagels wrote:
> As I said previously, I'm gathering material for a paper on NetHack and 
> its community.  One of the things I'm finding that I'd like to focus on 
> is the twin questions of spoilers and save-scumming.

I'll try to answer but, frankly, I'm unsure I understood what you were
talking about when you mention policies (or some other issues).


> I know save-scumming is something of a touchy issue; what I'm 
> particularly curious about is how the anti-scumming policy came about, 
> and what folks' stances are on it.  I believe I've even seen some folks 
> mention that they play exclusively on NAO to avoid the possible 
> temptation of scumming.

Must have been me; I mentioned it twice in the last week. Let me say
(so that nothing wrong will enter your article!), it was exaggerated.

At times when Nethack versions were not that stable as they are now
I've written backup-wrappers to protect my games; and once (or twice?)
I used it after uncautious play to savescum (so much for the shrift :-).
Times have changed, though, Nethack is extreemly stable and there's no
need to make backups. (I am speaking just for me, other platforms may
be worse, but I doubt it.)

Sidenote: IMO you learn to master the game better if you try to solve
difficult situations, and not mentally be assured that you can restore
any game. So save-scumming is counterproductive.

The question is, why do save scumming at all?
If you want to test the game there's the explore mode (or even wizard
mode) where you won't die; but also don't get into the highscore list.
If you want to get into the highscore table, well, you may simply use
a text editor and add your fictive entries to the file.
If you want to get famous being a good player you might want to post
here about your success; but you don't even need to play to write a
fictive story.
So there's no real point in save scumming. The cheater cheats himself
and no one else. It's his personal problem if he cheats. Thus I don't
quite understand the term "policy". But if one feels the necessity to
be a smart cheater and tries tricking the audience here he must expect
his lies to be revealed by inconsistencies or errors and to be branded
for the future.

Having successful games on NAO, OTOH, is one way to get some reputation.
Other ways are giving useful tips, writing interesting game stories,
writing patches or utilitie, or providing spoilers... and we've managed
to lead over to your next question, spoilers.

> 
> As for spoilers, the situation is the reverse, from what I can tell: 
> it's generally socially acceptable to ask for help or use the spoilers 
> to succeed (or at least survive) in NetHack. 

Yes. To understand that one needs, I think, to consider some points.
A played character would, strictly speaking, take his discoveries with
him into his grave, but the real life player keeps all knowledge in
memory (as far as memory serves :-).

At the point when one detects that prices are quite constant he may
memorize that a scroll of idenfity costs around 20 but he may as well
say, why memorizing all that, I look it up and keep my real life memory
free for other things.

But there is knowledge that is not that apparent or even completely
hidden from the player; internal information that you cannot quantify
or cannot even detect at all without having information from the source
code or from spoilers (which often depend on the source code).

I think it's personal preference whether to use that information or not.
My personal preference is to avoid exploiting non-apparent knowledge
even if I know of its existance, or know the concrete facts (the latter
is mostly hard to ignore).

